# ============================================================================
# Analysis script: sector-level rates and rank uncertainty,
# OSHA Severe Injury Reports (United States, federal jurisdiction)
#
# Usage   : run the script from top to bottom. It is safe to re-run:
#           finished parts are skipped and nothing is downloaded twice.
# Setup   : edit `base_candidates` in step00 so that it points to your own
#           working folder; the project folders are created inside it.
# Data    : public sources, downloaded by the script
#           - OSHA Severe Injury Reports
#           - BLS Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages (QCEW)
#           - reporting counts by sector from published Michigan linkage
#             studies, entered in the script as constants
# Requires: R with the data.table package; packages listed in step00 are
#           installed on first run if missing.
# Output  : Excel summaries (csv files as a fallback) and figures (PDF, PNG)
#           in the output folder of the project.
# Seeds   : random seeds are fixed, so results are reproducible.
#
# Contents
# step00 : setup (folders, packages, helpers)
# step01 : A) download + read OSHA Severe Injury Reports (SIR)
#          B) write the SIR summary Excel
#          C) download BLS QCEW employment (state x industry x year)
#          D) add the QCEW sheets to the same Excel
# step02 : federal-jurisdiction denominators, crude rates and ranks
# step03 : Monte Carlo rank uncertainty under industry-specific under-reporting
# step04 : tipping points, prior-free rank bounds, restricted-sector analysis
# step05 : hospitalization outcome + agriculture exposure sensitivity
# step06 : main tables and figures (PDF + PNG, written to the output folder)
# step07 : robustness checks (unit of analysis, all 19 sectors, weaker
#          Michigan pattern, sample flow, sector 21 content, suppressed cells, periods)
# step08 : count uncertainty without the Poisson assumption (over-dispersion,
#          resampling of years), rank bounds with count uncertainty, two reference quantities
# step09 : hierarchical (beta-binomial) reporting model, ranks without 2020-2021,
#          wider hospitalization definition, bounds for 15 sectors, the six other reports
# ============================================================================

